<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The resource-server half: the RFC 9728 metadata endpoints (E-22/E-23) and the bearer
    middleware that turns a missing or bad access token into a challenge (X-32..X-35).

    THERE IS NO REFERENCE TO Boltway.AuthorizationServer, AND THAT ABSENCE IS THE DESIGN.

    A resource server and an authorization server are separate deployables. The RS-only
    customer takes this package and never installs the AS; a project reference would drag a
    grant store, a signing key ring and a consent pipeline into a process that must not have
    any of them, and would make "these two can be deployed apart" a claim rather than a fact
    about the dependency graph.

    What they do share is the wire format, and that is exactly what the two references below
    are: Primitives fixes WWW-Authenticate quoting, resource identity and ordinal comparison;
    Tokens fixes the access-token profile (typ, alg, aud). Sharing the compiled code is what
    makes an AS/RS disagreement about any of those impossible rather than merely tested.

    Not multi-targeted, and the reason written here was wrong. docs/DESIGN.md 2 asks for
    net8.0;net10.0 on this package, and this said the blocker was the net8.0 targeting pack in CI
    plus a second matrix leg. Measured 2026-08-23 on SDK 10.0.111: the targeting pack restores from
    nuget.org with no CI change at all, and an ASP.NET Core net8.0 library that uses
    FrameworkReference builds here today. So the packaging half is free.

    What actually blocks it is an API. Boltway.OAuth.Primitives wraps
    System.Buffers.Text.Base64Url, which is .NET 9 and later; compiling that assembly against
    net8.0 fails with CS0234 on all three call sites. Multi-targeting therefore means a
    hand-written unpadded base64url behind a #if for net8.0 - in the primitive that encodes PKCE
    verifiers, jti values and JWK thumbprints, where the padding rule is byte-exact and getting it
    wrong produces a PKCE mismatch on every request with an error that says nothing about padding.
    (CA1307 is a second, smaller one: string.IndexOf(char, StringComparison) is also .NET 9+, and
    the analyzer is an error here.)

    That is a decision about writing crypto-adjacent code, not a packaging chore, so it is not
    taken on the way past. README's "Supported target frameworks" says the same thing to a
    consumer, which is who needs it: an MCP server on net8.0 LTS cannot reference this at all. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>
      The resource-server half: the RFC 9728 protected-resource metadata endpoints and the bearer
      middleware that turns a missing or bad access token into a challenge. It does not reference
      Boltway.AuthorizationServer, and that absence is the design — a resource server validates tokens
      and never issues them, so the two remain separate deployables.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives\Boltway.OAuth.Primitives.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Tokens\Boltway.OAuth.Tokens.csproj" />

    <!--
      For JwksSigningKeysExtensions, which points this package's SigningKeySource at an issuer's
      published key set. It moved here from Boltway.Mcp in 0.4.0 for a reason written on the class:
      it is resource-server wiring with nothing MCP about it, and filing it next door meant the one
      consumer who needed it wrote it again by hand instead.

      THIS EDGE ADDS NO PACKAGE A RESOURCE SERVER DID NOT ALREADY HAVE. Boltway.OAuth.Net's own
      dependencies are Boltway.OAuth.Primitives and Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens, both of which
      are already above. What it adds is the guarded HTTP transport - as an assembly, not as a
      running client: nothing is registered, no socket is opened and no URL is read until a
      deployment calls AddJwksSigningKeys. A resource server that fills SigningKeys by hand is
      exactly as it was.

      That is the same question OAuth.Net's own csproj answers about Boltway.OAuth.Tokens and
      answers the other way, and the difference is who compiles against what. Tokens is the one
      assembly BOTH halves of the product use, so a transport there would reach every authorization
      server too. This package is compiled against by resource servers alone, and fetching a JWKS is
      what most of them do.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Boltway.OAuth.Net\Boltway.OAuth.Net.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Used directly: JsonWebTokenHandler to validate, SecurityKey to hold the verification
         keys. They arrive transitively through Tokens anyway; naming them here means a future
         change to Tokens' dependencies cannot silently remove them from under this code. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A second enforcement scope for the normalizing members of System.Uri, for the same reason
    Primitives has one and independently of it: the ban list is per-project, so Primitives'
    list does not reach this assembly.

    The value at stake here is the resource identifier. RFC 9728 3.3 requires the `resource`
    member of the metadata document to be byte-identical to the identifier the client inserted
    the well-known suffix into, and 6 forbids Unicode normalization at any point. Every banned
    member below returns a value that has been through at least one normalization, so building
    either the document or the metadata URL out of one produces bytes the client compares and
    rejects - and the failure surfaces on the client as a generic "couldn't connect", with the
    server's own log showing a clean 200.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Boltway.ResourceServer.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
